{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Esau took his wives from the daughters of Canaan</b> etc. Here Bat Be'eri is not mentioned at all, while in the section of Toldot we find that Esau took her as a wife. One may answer that she died without sons, while the others listed here bore children. <b>Adah daughter of Elon.</b> This is Basemat daughter of Elon in the section of Toldot — her name changed. Likewise the daughter of Yishmael, who is called here Basemat and in the section of Toldot Machalat." "<b>Aholivamah daughter of Anah.</b> Rashi's comment: this is Yehudit. And if you ask: but Yehudit was the daughter of Be'eri, as written in the section of Toldot, while here it says daughter of Anah? And if there is in her something of mamzerut, why does Rashi not explain it — that she came out from both, from Be'eri and from Anah, just as he explained regarding Anah daughter of Tziv'on? One may answer: Be'eri and Anah are one and the same." "<b>Daughter of Anah daughter of Tziv'on.</b> In this perasha you will find Anah the brother of Tziv'on, as written, 'and Shoval and Tziv'on and Anah,' and it is written, 'and these are the sons of Tziv'on: Ayah and Anah' — this is what Rashi explained, that Tziv'on lay with his mother and begot from her Anah; if so, he was his son and his brother, and therefore he counts him both among the sons of Se'ir and among the sons of Tziv'on. And that is what is written, 'he is Anah' — meaning: he is the same Anah as before. And it is possible to say <b>daughter of Anah</b> son of Tziv'on, and what is written <b>daughter of Tziv'on</b> is not attached to Anah, for he was a male, but is attached to Aholivamah — meaning, <b>Aholivamah</b> who was the daughter of Anah and <b>daughter of Tziv'on</b>, for grandsons are like sons, as in the case of 'Bat Matri Bat Mi-Zahav.' One may say in the chapter Yesh Nochalin: Anah was a female, since it is written <b>daughter of Anah daughter of Tziv'on</b>. And as for what is written further on, 'he is Anah who found the yemim,' which implies a male — that was because she inherited in place of Ayah, for Ayah was the brother of Anah, and we do not find him counted among the chieftains; from this we may infer that Ayah died and Anah remained alone to her father, and therefore they counted her among the sons of Se'ir the Horite, the inhabitants of the land, and she inherited in the male's place and was counted among their chieftains."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {1 "The phrase ואם יש בה ממזרות אמאי לא פרש״י is read as a follow-up question; פרש״י taken as 'Rashi explained.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:37.770852642Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}